# CloakCal — web app environment.
#
# Copy to .env.local. Everything in the first block is PUBLIC: the publishable key
# identifies the project and authorises nothing. Row Level Security is what authorises, and
# it is enforced in Postgres regardless of what a client sends.
#
# THERE IS NO SERVICE-ROLE KEY IN THIS FILE, AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE. The service role
# bypasses RLS entirely, so a single careless query with it would hand one user another
# user's rows. Every read and write in the app runs as the signed-in user.
#
# THE BILLING BLOCK BELOW IS NOT A COUNTER-EXAMPLE TO THAT, AND IT IS WORTH BEING PRECISE,
# because this file is exactly where somebody will look for permission to add the service
# key. `BILLING_DATABASE_URL` connects as `billing_writer`, a role that holds privileges on
# `subscriptions` and `billing_events` and on NOTHING ELSE — it cannot read `workspaces`,
# `events`, `cloaked_fields` or `auth.users`, and `packages/db/test/billing-writer.test.ts`
# proves that by running AS the role. It is the narrow single-table role ADR 0007 designed
# INSTEAD of a service key, not a step toward one. The rule stands: no credential in this
# system can read or rewrite everything.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Development only. Set to 1 to serve the committed fixture instead of Supabase and to
# unlock it with the published development seed. Refused outright in a production build:
# NODE_ENV is inlined at build time, so setting this on a production server does nothing.
#
# One flag governs both the data and the key, so there is no configuration in which the app
# serves fixture data it cannot decrypt, or reaches for the development key against real rows.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLOAKCAL_DEV_UNLOCK=1

# --- Billing (Stripe) -------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE FIRST SERVER-ONLY SECRETS IN THIS REPO. None of these may ever gain a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix: that would inline the value into a browser bundle, silently.
#
# Get all six from `pnpm billing:setup`, which creates the Stripe objects over the API and
# prints them. See tools/stripe-setup.env.example for what that script needs.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED AS A SET. `billingConfig()` returns null unless EVERY one is present and
# well-formed, so a half-configured deployment renders no purchase control at all rather
# than a button whose route 500s. `CLOAKCAL_BILLING` alone is never enough.
#
# TEST MODE IS ENFORCED, NOT SUGGESTED. A key that is not `sk_test_` DISABLES billing rather
# than enabling it, which is how CLAUDE.md's "nothing takes real money before the independent
# security review" is a property of the build instead of a note somebody has to remember.
#
# THE FOUR SECRETS MUST BE TYPED **Sensitive** ON VERCEL, which means `vercel env pull`
# returns the literal string [SENSITIVE] for them while still printing a success line and
# exiting 0. `billingConfig()` refuses that string on purpose. That trap has already cost
# this project once on the Supabase values above, where a placeholder sailed past a
# loud-failure guard and surfaced much later as an inexplicable network error.
#
# CLOAKCAL_BILLING=1
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=price_...
# STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_ANNUAL=price_...
# STRIPE_PORTAL_CONFIGURATION_ID=bpc_...
#
# The pooler on port 6543, with the CUSTOM ROLE as the username. Not the direct connection
# host (db.<ref>.supabase.co is IPv6-only and a Vercel function cannot reach it), and not
# the `postgres` role, which billingConfig() refuses by prefix. Verify it before the first
# checkout rather than after, because a wrong one fails only AFTER somebody has paid:
#
#   psql "$BILLING_DATABASE_URL" -c "select current_user, current_setting('is_superuser')"
#   -> billing_writer | off
#
# BILLING_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://billing_writer.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-N-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?sslmode=require
